WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:01.630
MAX SALTONSTALL: Have you
ever wanted to be a hacker?

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.088
Have you ever
thought about how you

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:06.610
would gain access to a system
you weren't supposed to?

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.000
Well, in this
video, you're going

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.060
to see how you can make
Add a Cat to That more

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.440
secure by donning
your own attacker hat

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.329
and trying to break it.

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:18.640
When it comes to security,
you don't just flip a switch

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.680
and suddenly your app is secure.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.080
Instead of thinking
of it as yes or no,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.270
it's better to think
of it as a spectrum.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.790
Even with an app as simple
as Add a Cat to That,

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:29.966
we've got a lot of work
to keep our application

00:00:29.966 --> 00:00:32.049
from becoming one of those
security horror stories

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:33.070
you read about.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.630
There's a few ways we
can make some progress.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:39.730
The first step is putting
on our attacker hat

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.567
and doing an exercise
called threat modeling.

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.650
In threat modeling, you
take advantage of the fact

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:48.280
that you are the best person
to attack your software.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.680
You have the most
in-depth knowledge of it.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.080
Then you use those
possible attack methods

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.828
to make your
application more secure.

00:00:55.828 --> 00:00:57.370
Remember, an attacker
is probably not

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.710
going to just randomly
poke at your software.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.670
They have a goal in mind.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.019
This might be your user data,
financial information you have,

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:08.050
or the goal could be something
outside your organization.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.550
Perhaps, they want
to take advantage

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:13.180
of an open source library you
maintain that other people use.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.700
Whatever the goal, they won't
be able to get straight at it,

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.210
because if it's
important to you,

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.330
it's probably pretty
well protected.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.200
So an attacker is going to
look for intermediate resources

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.785
as stepping stones.

00:01:23.785 --> 00:01:25.660
They're going to
investigate your environment

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:26.890
and try to find
different pieces they

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.570
can take advantage
of to make it further

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.633
into your infrastructure.

00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:32.050
These intermediate
resources could

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.140
be servers running in a cluster,
libraries you depend on,

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.780
or even your
developers themselves.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.540
As your attackers
investigate, they're

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.998
going to try to learn about
any defensive measures you have

00:01:41.998 --> 00:01:45.340
in place, password policies,
auditing practices, firewall

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.003
rules, and the like.

00:01:47.003 --> 00:01:49.420
They're probably going to start
the process of enumerating

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.310
all these things
from information

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.170
they can find online.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:55.960
And they might find a way to
use all that to wiggle around

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.560
your defensive measures to get
access to information, or plant

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.530
malware, or other
nefarious things.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.260
This series of steps leading
to a successful attack

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.770
is called the kill chain.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.300
And it's our job to stop it.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.732
Our first impulse after
figuring out a kill chain

00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:15.690
is I want to take one of
our defensive measures

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.140
and just beef it up.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.390
Add more firewall rules.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.310
Segment the network.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.200
Instead, focus on
reducing the attack

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.510
surface of the pieces you use.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.460
That means removing
unnecessary components

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.010
and simplifying services and
APIs to reduce an attacker's

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.290
possible entry points.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.510
And rather than having one
strong measure in place,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.080
it's more effective to
employ defense in depth.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.830
Use a greater number
of defensive measures

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.480
that overlap.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.910
That way if one thing
fails for whatever reason,

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.060
you have another backup
behind it to protect you.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.650
The big idea?

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.410
Build up your defenses in
layers so that the attacker has

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.410
to cry as they peel the onion.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.360
Now that you've seen
the defensive strategy,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.040
be sure to subscribe
below to see

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.740
how you can use it to
protect your supply chain,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.540
automatically discover
vulnerabilities,

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.040
and minimize phishing.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.580
Thanks for watching
and for letting

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.200
me rock this excellent hat.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

